          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Specialist rule of thumb: \"The most common cause of blue smoke on a high-mileage petrol engine is hardened valve seals from infrequent oil changes. The most common cause of white smoke is a head gasket failure from an overheating event - often a stuck thermostat or failed water pump. The most common cause of black smoke on a diesel is a clogged injector from poor fuel quality. Regular maintenance prevents all three.\""
